WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.247 --> 00:00:03.052
- "GEICO ordered to pay
Missouri woman 5.2 million

00:00:03.052 --> 00:00:06.007
after she contracted STD in car."

00:00:06.007 --> 00:00:09.172
"GEICO must pay 5.2 million
to a woman who got HPV

00:00:09.172 --> 00:00:12.420
from sex in man's insured
car, court rules."

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.568
Well, I'm sorry to tell you
that the headlines are wrong,

00:00:15.568 --> 00:00:17.280
but the truth is even more interesting

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.283
because it involves nuances

00:00:19.283 --> 00:00:22.830
and esoteric details of insurance law.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:23.663
Now, you may have heard

00:00:23.663 --> 00:00:25.320
that GEICO was on the hook for 5 million

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.530
because a woman contracted HPV
after she had sex with a man

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:32.400
in his 2014 Hyundai Genesis
that was insured by GEICO.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.053
And it is true that a thing happened

00:00:35.053 --> 00:00:38.400
that a lawsuit was filed
between the former couple.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.920
The man knew he apparently
had throat cancer

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.080
that was caused by HPV

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.030
but did not tell the woman that he had it.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:46.410
She took him to an arbitration

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.480
and was awarded more than 5 million.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.587
As Lawyer Elie Mystal put it,

00:00:50.587 --> 00:00:54.293
"That was probably an expensive
15 minutes for GEICO."

00:00:54.293 --> 00:00:57.270
(dramatic music)

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:58.230
Now, it occurs to me

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.810
that I probably should have
put a warning on this episode.

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:01.920
If there are any kids watching,

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.900
sex is when two people love each other

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.839
and then wrestle in the back of a car,

00:01:05.839 --> 00:01:08.820
in this case, a 2014 Hyundai Genesis.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.309
And when those two people
no longer love each other,

00:01:11.309 --> 00:01:16.080
they will sue each other for
what happened in that car.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.890
Now, how does that implicate
GEICO, you might ask?

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.110
Well, the man's car was insured by GEICO.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.787
The encounter happened in 2017,

00:01:23.787 --> 00:01:27.090
and the woman filed an insurance
claim, which GEICO denied.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:28.140
GEICO told the woman

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.090
it's auto insurance coverage didn't apply

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.950
because the damages claim did not arise

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.150
out of a normal use of the vehicle,

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.570
which I think we can all
agree, probably makes sense.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:38.580
And now to understand this whole case,

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:39.810
we have to take our first diversion

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.060
into the nuances of insurance law.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:42.960
Because generally speaking,

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.370
when you get an insurance policy,

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:48.450
it provides insurance over,
let's say an accident happening,

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.880
in occurrence of some
kind, but in addition,

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.250
you'll often get what's
called a duty to defend.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:54.870
In other words, if you are sued

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.420
over something that might
be covered by the policy,

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:00.436
then the insurance company
has to provide lawyers

00:02:00.436 --> 00:02:04.367
or at least the funds for
lawyers to defend that case.

00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:07.680
♪ Send lawyers, guns and money ♪

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.150
Now, when an insurance company refuses

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:10.680
to defend its policy holder,

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.150
it's considered basically the same

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.800
as denying coverage outright,

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.630
saying that the policy doesn't cover

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:16.980
the accident that happened.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.130
So an insurance company
that wrongfully refuses

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:21.690
to defend its insured can be liable

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.180
for the cost of the defense
in any damages that arise

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.100
from the breach of their duty to defend.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:26.984
Now, how do you know

00:02:26.984 --> 00:02:29.760
if a policy is going to
apply to an accident,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.420
or whether the insurance company
has a duty to defend you?

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.300
Well, you look to the four corners

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.620
of the complaint in the lawsuit,

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.590
and you compare that to the policy itself.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.060
This is called the four corners rule,

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.290
or sometimes the eight corners.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:44.850
You're comparing what's contained

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.466
inside the lawsuit complaint

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:48.570
to what's contained inside the policy.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:52.494
And if it's possible, if
it's a remote possibility

00:02:52.494 --> 00:02:56.580
that this claim could be
covered by the insurance policy,

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:57.480
then the insurance company

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.730
has to provide you with a defense.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:01.669
Now, if the allegations are untrue,

00:03:01.669 --> 00:03:04.350
then the insurance company
won't have to pay a judgment

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.192
because well, they'll win.

00:03:06.192 --> 00:03:08.640
But you can't know that
until the trial is over,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.980
which is why the insured
needs someone to pay

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:12.000
for their defense.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.160
And that's why there's
often a duty to defend

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.630
under certain policies.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.370
And that's why sometimes the defense costs

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:18.240
are the whole ballgame.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.490
If the insurance company
denies the defense,

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:22.890
then the insured might
be in a world of trouble.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.470
They have to defend against
a lawsuit all on their own,

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:28.380
and lawyers are expensive.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.217
Now, an old partner of
mine used to say that,

00:03:30.217 --> 00:03:32.040
"The only thing an
insurance policy gets you

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.930
is the right to sue an insurance company."

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:37.740
In other words, if they deny
your defense or deny coverage,

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.190
then you might have to
sue the insurance company

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:42.878
if you lose the initial
case going forward.

00:03:42.878 --> 00:03:45.150
But that's why some plaintiff's attorneys

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:48.047
will actually specifically
try to cast their allegations

00:03:48.047 --> 00:03:50.922
in a way that will invoke
insurance coverage,

00:03:50.922 --> 00:03:52.740
so that they make sure
the insurance company

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.180
is part of the litigation.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.130
They'll either add another claim,

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:57.660
or they will craft the story

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.496
of what happened in a way that might fall

00:03:59.496 --> 00:04:01.590
under coverage of the insurance company

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:02.970
because the insurance companies

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.490
tend to be the ones that
have the big pockets.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:08.171
So now we come back to
this particular case.

00:04:08.171 --> 00:04:11.640
In February of 2021, the woman sent GEICO

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.944
a copy of a lawsuit that
she was about to file

00:04:13.944 --> 00:04:15.120
against the Insured,

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.400
the guy that she had sex with in the car.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.010
She offered to settle
the claim for $1 million.

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:21.240
GEICO ignored the notice.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:23.910
Basically, GEICO decided
that the claim wasn't covered

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:25.440
and refused to defend.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.318
Basically, GEICO said, "Having
sex in a car that we ensure

00:04:28.318 --> 00:04:30.030
is not covered under the policy,

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:32.190
so we're not going to provide coverage,

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:33.552
and we're not going to provide a defense

00:04:33.552 --> 00:04:36.210
to the guy whose car it was."

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:37.043
And for the record,

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:40.440
these people only use their
initials in the lawsuit,

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.260
which was probably an
incredibly smart decision

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:44.804
so that they don't go and down in history

00:04:44.804 --> 00:04:47.610
for something that may
or may not have happened

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:48.990
in this particular suit.

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:51.930
And for the record, M.O.
is the injured party.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:53.578
She's the one that contracted HPV.

00:04:53.578 --> 00:04:58.170
M.B. is the man with a
car who gave her HPV.

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:00.720
So now we're in 2021,

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:04.200
M.B. is going to defend
this lawsuit on his own dime

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.690
because GEICO has refused
to provide a defense.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:09.660
So GEICO basically takes
itself out of the picture.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.580
At which point, the Insured, M.B.,

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:14.190
the guy with the car and the STI,

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:15.734
enter into an arbitration agreement

00:05:15.734 --> 00:05:18.450
with M.O. in March of 2021.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.940
And they agree to settle their
dispute through arbitration.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:23.730
Now, Missouri's insurance laws
have some procedural quirks,

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:27.510
and the relevant statute
is section 537.065.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:30.000
That statute allows a claimant, like M.O.,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.640
to enter into a contract
with the tort-feasor here,

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:34.710
M.B., the guy with the HPV,

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:36.750
to settle their dispute
in private arbitration,

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:38.250
which would be much, much cheaper

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:41.580
and much, much quicker than
going to trial in state court.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:43.401
Now, you're allowed to enter
into one of these agreements

00:05:43.401 --> 00:05:46.530
when the insurance company has
refused to defend the insured

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:48.120
or agrees to a defense of honor

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.350
under a reservation of rights,

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:50.183
which I won't go into.

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:52.409
Now, the parties enter
arbitration with an agreement

00:05:52.409 --> 00:05:55.286
that the maximum payout
that the arbitrator can give

00:05:55.286 --> 00:05:58.200
is whatever the insurance
policy limits are.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.450
And generally, that the plaintiff

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:03.268
won't go after the
defendant, the tort-feasor,

00:06:03.268 --> 00:06:05.017
and will just take the judgment

00:06:05.017 --> 00:06:06.840
and use it against the insurance company,

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.390
which as discussed, depends
on whether the claim

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:10.440
is covered or not.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.038
Effectively, the plaintiff is gambling

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:15.125
that the claim is or
should have been covered.

00:06:15.125 --> 00:06:17.070
And if they win in arbitration,

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:18.667
they're going to take that award and say,

00:06:18.667 --> 00:06:19.710
"You, insurance company,

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:22.710
you should have covered your insured.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:25.980
And, therefore, you can just
pay me everything that was owed

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:27.810
because we made this side agreement."

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:30.300
So in other words, once you
get an award in arbitration,

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.550
either the insured sues
the insurance company

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.285
and turns that money over to the claimant,

00:06:34.285 --> 00:06:36.863
or the claimant standing
in the shoes of the insured

00:06:36.863 --> 00:06:38.580
sues the insurance company saying

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:40.950
that they wrongfully denied
coverage or defense costs,

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:42.330
that's called subrogation.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:44.658
Now, obviously, insurance
companies hated all of this

00:06:44.658 --> 00:06:46.740
and that's why this section
has been amended twice,

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.200
once in 2017 and once in 2021.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.360
And this is effectively
exactly what happened here.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.280
They went to arbitration without GEICO,

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:55.020
'cause GEICO said they
didn't wanna participate,

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:56.160
they didn't think it was covered,

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:58.863
and the claimant got an
award of $5.2 million.

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:01.050
And the arbitrator found,

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:03.420
and yes, this is the actual language

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:04.559
that the arbitrator had to find

00:07:04.559 --> 00:07:07.890
because law is interesting.

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:10.410
The arbitrator found, quote,
"there was sexual activity

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:13.350
in Insured's automobile in
November/December of 2017

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:15.420
which occurred in Jackson
County, Missouri; 2,

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:17.370
these sexual activity in Insured's vehicle

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:19.290
'directly caused, or directly contributed

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:22.560
to M.O. to be infected with HPV';

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:25.361
3, Insured knew or had
been told his throat cancer

00:07:25.361 --> 00:07:27.629
was diagnosed as HPV positive;

00:07:27.629 --> 00:07:30.210
4, Insured should have
disclosed his diagnosis

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:32.113
to M.O. prior to their sexual
activity that occurred,

00:07:32.113 --> 00:07:34.050
and he did not; and 5,

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:36.060
Insured was negligent and is liable

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.173
for causing M.O. to contract HPV.

00:07:38.173 --> 00:07:40.590
The arbitrator found that
the 'amount that would fairly

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:42.210
and justly compensate Plaintiff, M.O.,

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:45.540
for all of her damages and
injuries is $5.2 million'

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:47.040
and entered an award in that amount

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:48.480
'in favor of Plaintiff, M.O.,

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.250
and against the Defendant, the Insured.'"

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:51.083
So in the end,

00:07:51.083 --> 00:07:54.000
the arbitrator awarded her
5.2 million in damages.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.980
And so the headlines were sort of right,

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:58.530
that M.O. did get a
judgment for 5.2 million

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:00.600
against the guy that screwed her.

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:02.545
Pun absolutely intended.

00:08:02.545 --> 00:08:03.660
(bomb exploding)

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:06.150
But here, it just wasn't against GEICO.

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:08.520
Yet, GEICO asserted that
the plaintiff and defendant

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.424
had conspired to jack up the damages award

00:08:10.424 --> 00:08:11.803
in the arbitration.

00:08:11.803 --> 00:08:14.580
M.O.'s counsel says that
this wasn't the situation

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:15.828
in their case, that the liability

00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:18.000
and damages were determined on the merits,

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:20.280
and the arbitration
proceeding was not based

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:22.830
on any admissions and that
it was fairly contested.

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:26.400
There were arguments and
witnesses going both ways.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.650
But after this arbitration award,

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:31.680
the woman informed the
Insured that she would file

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:35.280
an action in state court to
confirm the damages award,

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:36.113
which is what you do.

00:08:36.113 --> 00:08:37.230
When you get an arbitration award,

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:39.491
you then basically
confirm it in state court.

00:08:39.491 --> 00:08:42.180
Now, at this point,
GEICO tried to intervene.

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:43.230
They wanted a new trial

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:45.930
because they didn't think
the arbitration was fair.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:47.460
That motion was denied.

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:49.920
But at the same time, in April of 2021,

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.620
GEICO filed a separate
suit in federal court

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:55.437
in the district of Kansas
seeking a determination

00:08:55.437 --> 00:08:57.870
that it has no duty to defend

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:01.087
or indemnify M.B., the guy with HPV.

00:09:01.087 --> 00:09:03.960
So GEICO chose to fight
its case in two fronts,

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:06.090
one in the state court
and one in federal court.

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:07.410
And it's really the federal court case

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:09.060
that's going to determine on the merits,

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:11.668
whether GEICO actually
has to provide a defense

00:09:11.668 --> 00:09:14.850
or provide indemnity based
on the contract language.

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:16.650
We'll come back to that in a second.

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:18.712
Now, in state court, GEICO
argued that the judgment,

00:09:18.712 --> 00:09:21.895
the arbitration, violated the
company's due process rights,

00:09:21.895 --> 00:09:24.360
and the arbitration
agreement was unenforceable.

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:26.496
GEICO said it never had an
opportunity to participate

00:09:26.496 --> 00:09:29.910
in discovery or to provide
a meaningful defense.

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:31.800
And by the time that they
were aware of the judgment,

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:33.570
they argued it was too late.

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:34.980
And you can see some merit to this.

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:36.930
They didn't get a chance
to contest the facts

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:39.060
in this more or less secret arbitration,

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:41.250
where the Claimant and the
Insured might have colluded

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:42.570
to drive up the award.

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:44.700
And at the very least,
the Insured didn't care

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:47.610
because, by virtue of the
agreement that he made,

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:50.280
he wasn't on the hook for the damages.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:51.600
So he might not have colluded,

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.420
but he also didn't care if
there was this giant judgment

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:56.490
because the Plaintiff, M.O.,

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:59.130
was just gonna turn around
and sue GEICO over it.

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:02.443
Now, on the other hand, this
arbitration and this award,

00:10:02.443 --> 00:10:04.200
it's not against GEICO,

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:07.530
it's an award against M.B., the HPV guy.

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:10.213
If GEICO was right that the
policy doesn't cover it,

00:10:10.213 --> 00:10:12.180
then they don't have to pay.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:13.740
And we'll talk about that in a second,

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:14.880
whether the policy covers it.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:17.112
And if GEICO wanted to participate

00:10:17.112 --> 00:10:18.690
in this from the very beginning

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:22.020
and they were worried that they
would have to pay indemnity

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:23.987
to M.B. or cover his defense costs,

00:10:23.987 --> 00:10:26.430
then they could have
provided him a defense

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:27.690
from the very beginning.

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:29.550
That's the point of the duty to defend.

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:31.740
And that's why state
laws are often crafted

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:33.300
in a way to incentivize
insurance companies

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:35.040
to provide a defense.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:37.063
Because you can't go back in time

00:10:37.063 --> 00:10:39.090
and provide a defense after the fact.

00:10:39.090 --> 00:10:41.341
So if you, the insurance
company, are worried

00:10:41.341 --> 00:10:44.280
that you might have to
cover the damages award,

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:46.140
well, then provide a defense to the guy

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:47.378
that you provided insurance

00:10:47.378 --> 00:10:50.185
and participate throughout
the entire thing.

00:10:50.185 --> 00:10:52.267
As the Missouri court found, quote,

00:10:52.267 --> 00:10:54.270
"GEICO's claim of
constitutional deprivations

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:56.240
presumes GEICO was denied the opportunity

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.020
to defend its interest relating

00:10:58.020 --> 00:10:59.910
to M.O.'s claims against Insured,

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:02.940
including the ability to
challenge liability and damages.

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:05.223
But GEICO did have the
opportunity to participate

00:11:05.223 --> 00:11:06.720
and defend its interests,

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:09.120
including the ability to
challenge liability and damages,

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:11.490
by entering a defense of the Insured.

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:13.680
GEICO did not take advantage
of this opportunity,

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:16.170
and instead denied coverage
and refused to defend Insured.

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:19.350
Moreover, GEICO initiated a
declaratory judgment action

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:21.240
in federal court seeking a determination

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:23.175
as to its obligations
under the insurance policy,

00:11:23.175 --> 00:11:25.499
and that federal action
provides yet another forum

00:11:25.499 --> 00:11:28.454
in which GEICO may defend its interest."

00:11:28.454 --> 00:11:31.170
And right, the federal court action.

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:33.690
GEICO is currently suing in federal court

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:35.970
for an order that says
the policy doesn't cover

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:38.070
the HPV transmission at all.

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:40.620
And if they win, they
won't have to pay anything,

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:42.300
no part of the 5.2 million,

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:45.390
because that judgment is only against M.B.

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:46.740
So the headlines were wrong.

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:48.917
There has been no determination
that GEICO was responsible

00:11:48.917 --> 00:11:52.710
for this 5.2 million award, for now.

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:53.850
There's also no determination

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:56.478
that GEICO wrongfully
denied a defense to M.B.,

00:11:56.478 --> 00:11:58.470
but they might.

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:00.510
Now, usually if someone
files an insurance claim

00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:01.980
because they were injured in a car,

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:03.390
it's because of an accident.

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:04.950
That's one of the reasons that this seems

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:06.360
like an unfair ruling.

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:08.220
It puts insurance companies
in a weird position

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:09.600
of defending any and all claims,

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:11.580
no matter how bonkers they might seem.

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:12.510
But it's really a question

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:14.790
of whether the insurance
policy actually applies

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:15.960
to this scenario.

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:17.408
GEICO asserted that there's no way policy

00:12:17.408 --> 00:12:20.190
can be interpreted to include
this kind of negligence.

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:23.460
The woman sued over the
contracting an STI in a car.

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:24.390
But to determine that,

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:27.150
we have to look at the policy language.

00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:29.250
Generally, since insurance
companies write these policies,

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:31.263
the language is construed against them.

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:33.390
Let me know if you've ever had a chance

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:36.039
to write a custom insurance endorsement.

00:12:36.039 --> 00:12:37.920
Generally, the insurance company

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:39.130
gives you what they give you,

00:12:39.130 --> 00:12:41.799
which is why policies are
often hundreds of pages long.

00:12:41.799 --> 00:12:45.290
They're trying to prevent
any and all weird situation.

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:49.470
So in this particular case,
what does the policy say?

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:51.000
Well, I had to do some digging,

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:52.710
but I found the relevant language

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:54.480
in GEICO's appellate brief.

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:57.607
According to GEICO, the
policy covers bodily injuries

00:12:57.607 --> 00:13:00.338
"arising out of the ownership,
operation, maintenance

00:13:00.338 --> 00:13:02.910
or use of a motor vehicle."

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:06.563
Now, you might notice, that
language is very broad.

00:13:06.563 --> 00:13:09.506
Does this type of injury arise

00:13:09.506 --> 00:13:11.310
out of the ownership of a car?

00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:12.900
Well, it seems like a lot

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:14.220
of stuff arises out of the ownership.

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:17.970
Does it qualify as use of
the motor vehicle? Maybe.

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:20.580
Is this a bodily injury? Maybe.

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:21.870
Is it the type of bodily injury

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:24.960
that's intended to be covered
by an insurance policy?

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:25.920
Who knows?

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:27.510
But for the most part, you're just looking

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:29.400
at the text and the meaning of the words

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:31.020
that are in the policy itself

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:33.484
construed against the insurance company

00:13:33.484 --> 00:13:37.800
or in the light that is more
favorable to the insured.

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:40.020
So it's possible that GEICO
might have to cover this,

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:41.639
because if it's even possible

00:13:41.639 --> 00:13:44.610
that this language covers this situation,

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:48.496
then GEICO was wrong to deny
defense to M.B., the Insured,

00:13:48.496 --> 00:13:50.010
and they're gonna be on the hook

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:51.420
for not only the attorney's fees,

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:53.850
but also the award of damages itself.

00:13:53.850 --> 00:13:54.750
But we'll know soon.

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:55.962
GEICO filed a lawsuit in federal court

00:13:55.962 --> 00:13:57.750
seeking a declaratory judgment

00:13:57.750 --> 00:13:59.940
that it is not responsible
for this situation.

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:02.160
And that is scheduled
for trial in October.

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:03.554
But even if GEICO loses,

00:14:03.554 --> 00:14:06.525
don't consider this a license
to engage in unsafe sex,

00:14:06.525 --> 00:14:07.590
because you can bet

00:14:07.590 --> 00:14:09.750
that GEICO has already
changed the policy language

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:11.720
in all of its car policies to make sure

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:16.260
that no future policies cover
this situation in the future.

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:17.093
But either way,

00:14:17.093 --> 00:14:19.230
it seems this is the new
McDonald's hot coffee case.

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:21.840
People completely misunderstood
what was happening

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:23.190
in this particular case.

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:24.240
But it is another example

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:25.890
of using your car for weird things.

